1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Aryeh Leib ben Saul Lowenstam (ca. 1690, Cracow – April 2, 1755, Amsterdam) was a Polish rabbi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Aryeh Leib came of a famous family of rabbis. His father Saul had been rabbi of Cracow from 1700 to 1704, his grandfather was Rabbi Hoeschl of Cracow. In 1707 in Berlin he married Miriam, the oldest daughter of Ẓebi Ashkenazi, the
2 CONC n rabbi in Altona, and continued his studies under his father-in-law, with whom he went to Amsterdam, and thence to Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first known rabbinical position was in Dubno. He was elected rabbi of Dukla in 1717. Through the influence of his relatives he then obtained the rabbinical position in Tarnopol in 1720[1] (or 1718?), the former incumbent havin
2 CONC g been ousted by the officials of the government to make room for him. This interference on the part of the civic authorities naturally aroused great opposition to him in the congregation, and Aryeh Loeb was deposed in 1724. Subse
2 CONC quently he was elected Rabbi of Rzeszów from 1724-1728. In 1728 he was appointed as Rabbi of both the towns of Glogau and Lviv, a position held until 1740. In 1740 he was appointed Rabbi of Amsterdam (a position that was offered t
2 CONC o his father Saul years earlier), a position he held until his death in 1755. A call was extended to him from Prague in 1751, but he did not accept it. While the Jewish Encyclopedia is doubtful of whether he was rabbi in Lviv, as 
2 CONC stated by Buber (Anshe Shem, p. 38), Dembitzer in the Klilat Yofi and Reuven Margaliot provide proof of his position in Lviv/Lemberg, with Dembitzer stating that he held both positions simultaneously, while Margaliot is of the opi
2 CONC nion that he changed positions a number of times in those years between the rabbinate of Glogau and Lviv.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT Aryeh did not publish any books, and what there is of his exists in the works of others—as in the responsa of Ẓebi Ashkenazi, No. 76; in those of Mordecai of Düsseldorf (Maamar Mordecai, Nos. 62, 63, Brünn, 1790), and in the works
2 CONC  of his son Saul, Binyan Ariel (Amsterdam, 1778)—and shows no originality. He took an active part in the controversy between Jacob Emden and Jonathan Eybeschütz, and sided with the former, who was his wife's brother. His letters o
2 CONC n that controversy are full of invectives against Eybeschütz (see Emden's Sefat Emet, p. 16, Lemberg, 1877). According to the testimony of his brother-in-law, Jacob Emden (see the latter's autobiography, Megillat Sefer, pp. 21, 68
2 CONC , Warsaw, 1896), he was a man of mediocre abilities, whose scientific attainments were not above the practical requirements for the rabbinical office.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his sons, one, Saul Aryeh, was his successor, while the other, who called himself Hirschel Lewin or Levin, was Chief rabbi in London and Berlin. The son of the latter was Chief rabbi Solomon Herschell, first Chief Rabbi of the 
2 CONC British Empire. Saul's daughter Sarah Leah was the wife of Yitzhak HaLevi, the rabbi of Kraków from 1776 till his death in 1799. Yitzhak HaLevi's son Tzvi Hirsch David Ha-Levi was Acting Rabbi of Kraków from 1799 and formally appo
2 CONC inted Rabbi of Kraków in 1816 till his death in 1831.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Aryeh Leib came of a famous family of rabbis. His father Saul had been rabbi of Cracow from 1700 to 1704, his grandfather was Rabbi Hoeschl of Cracow. In 1707 in Berlin he married Miriam, the oldest daughter of Ẓebi Ashkenazi, the
2 CONC n rabbi in Altona, and continued his studies under his father-in-law, with whom he went to Amsterdam, and thence to Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first known rabbinical position was in Dubno[1]. He was elected rabbi of Dukla in 1717[1]. Through the influence of his relatives he then obtained the rabbinical position in Tarnopol in 1720[1] (or 1718[citation needed]), the 
2 CONC former incumbent having been ousted by the officials of the government to make room for him. This interference on the part of the civic authorities naturally aroused great opposition to him in the congregation, and Aryeh Loeb was 
2 CONC deposed in 1724. Subsequently he was elected Rabbi of Rzeszów from 1724-1728[2]. In 1728 he was appointed as Rabbi of both the towns of Glogau and Lviv, a position held until 1740[2]. In 1740 he was appointed Rabbi of Amsterdam (a
2 CONC  position that was offered to his father Saul years earlier[2]), a position he held until his death in 1755. A call was extended to him from Prague in 1751, but he did not accept it. While the Jewish Encyclopedia is doubtful of wh
2 CONC ether he was rabbi in Lviv, as stated by Buber (Anshe Shem, p. 38), Dembitzer in the Klilat Yofi [2] and Reuven Margaliot [1] provide proof of his position in Lviv/Lemberg, with Dembitzer stating that he held both positions simult
2 CONC aneously, while Margaliot is of the opinion that he changed positions a number of times in those years between the rabbinate of Glogau and Lviv.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Works
2 CONT
2 CONT Aryeh did not publish any books, and what there is of his exists in the works of others—as in the responsa of Ẓebi Ashkenazi, No. 76; in those of Mordecai of Düsseldorf (Maamar Mordecai, Nos. 62, 63, Brünn, 1790), and in the works
2 CONC  of his son Saul, Binyan Ariel (Amsterdam, 1778)—and shows no originality. He took an active part in the controversy between Jacob Emden and Jonathan Eybeschütz, and sided with the former, who was his wife's brother. His letters o
2 CONC n that controversy are full of invectives against Eybeschütz (see Emden's Sefat Emet, p. 16, Lemberg, 1877). According to the testimony of his brother-in-law, Jacob Emden (see the latter's autobiography, Megillat Sefer, pp. 21, 68
2 CONC , Warsaw, 1896), he was a man of mediocre abilities, whose scientific attainments were not above the practical requirements for the rabbinical office.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his sons, one, Saul (1717 - 20 June, 1790), was his successor, while the other, who called himself Hirschel Lewin or Levin, was Chief rabbi in London and Berlin. The son of the latter was Chief rabbi Solomon Herschell, first Ch
2 CONC ief Rabbi of the British Empire. His daughter Sarah Leah was the wife of Yitzhak HaLevi, the rabbi of Kraków from 1776 till his death in 1799. Yitzhak HaLevi's son Tzvi Hirsch David Ha-Levi was Acting Rabbi of Kraków from 1799 and
2 CONC  formally appointed Rabbi of Kraków in 1816 till his death in 1831.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  LEIB Fishles Der Hoicher (The Tall) and Yuta's daughter Ester married Shaul LOWENSTAM son of Abraham Yehoshua Heschel (1596-1663).
2 CONT
2 CONT Chacham Zvi Ashkenazi They had a son Aryeh LEIB (1690-1756) who married Miriam ASHKENAZI the daughter of the Chacham Zvi ASHKENAZI and his wife Sara MIRLES. (See this 1700 painting of the Chacham Zvi on the right. One copy of this
2 CONC  painting is in the Jewish Mueseum in London. Another copy is in Jerusalem at Josh BUSH's father's cousin's house. A third copy is in the possession of a family in London, a relative of Paul SHAVIV. There is a great story around t
2 CONC his painting. It is probably the very first time that a Rabbi was actually painted. Since than, it became more acceptable. One story is that the Chacham Zvi was interviewing for a rabbinical position in London. Another story is th
2 CONC at he was called to London to settle a dispute amoung the Sephardim - possibly to check a controversial sermon preached by Chacham David Nieto for heresy. In any case, the door was left open a bit and seemingly, without his knowle
2 CONC dge the painter painted him. His son Yakov Emden (The YaBetz: Yakov Ben Zvi) writes about it in his very famous biography entitled Megilat Sefer.) A slightly different portrait of the Hakham Tzvi appears in M. H. GANS, Memorbook, 
2 CONC Baarn, 1977, p. 163 (top), together with the more famous copy in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zvi Hersch Berlin (Rabbi Hart Lion) The son of Arie LEIB and Miriam was Zvi Hersch Berlin (Rabbi Hart Lion) 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aryeh_Leib_ben_Saul
2 CONT --------------------
