1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Russian rabbi; born at Brest-Litovsk about 1770; died at Wilna Jan. 12, 1825. He was one of the four sons of Rabbi Joseph b. Abraham Katzenellenbogen of Brest-Litovsk. After living for some time in Polotzk, he went
2 CONC  to Wilna, and became a disciple of R. Elijah Gaon and one of the assistant rabbis of Wilna.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also a prominent communal leader, and the stories which are still related about him, especially in connection with the gaon, by the older inhabitants of Wilna bear testimony to his great popularity, which is made evident al
2 CONC so by the two special works about him that appeared after his death; "Ebel Kabed," by the poet A. B. Lebensohn (Wilna-Grodno, 1825), and "Gib'at Sha'ul," by a relative, Hirsch Katzenellenbogen (ib. 1825). R. Saul was a man of asce
2 CONC tic habits and ruined his health by much fasting. He is the author of annotations on the Babylonian Talmud, which appeared in the first Wilna edition and were republished in most of the subsequent editions. He wrote also several a
2 CONC pprobations of the works of others. Some of his novellæ appeared in Hirsch Katzenellenbogen's "Netibot 'Olam" (Wilna, 1822, 1858).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Russian rabbi; born at Brest-Litovsk about 1770; died at Wilna Jan. 12, 1825. He was one of the four sons of Rabbi Joseph b. Abraham Katzenellenbogen of Brest-Litovsk. After living for some time in Polotzk, he went to Wilna, and b
2 CONC ecame a disciple of R. Elijah Gaon and one of the assistant rabbis of Wilna.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also a prominent communal leader, and the stories which are still related about him, especially in connection with the gaon, by the older inhabitants of Wilna bear testimony to his great popularity, which is made evident al
2 CONC so by the two special works about him that appeared after his death; "Ebel Kabed," by the poet A. B. Lebensohn (Wilna-Grodno, 1825), and "Gib'at Sha'ul," by a relative, Hirsch Katzenellenbogen (ib. 1825). R. Saul was a man of asce
2 CONC tic habits and ruined his health by much fasting. He is the author of annotations on the Babylonian Talmud, which appeared in the first Wilna edition and were republished in most of the subsequent editions. He wrote also several a
2 CONC pprobations of the works of others. Some of his novellæ appeared in Hirsch Katzenellenbogen's "Netibot 'Olam" (Wilna, 1822, 1858).
2 CONT
2 CONT Read more: http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=135&letter=K#421#ixzz1FA5RxMcn
2 CONT
2 CONT The Unbroken Chain indicates that R' Saul Katzenellenbogen married 'Malka, daughter of Jacob' (3rd Edition, Volume 3, page 351)
2 CONT
