1 NOTE {geni:about_me} R. Saul Hirschel was born in 1740 when his father was nineteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was twenty he was ordained by some of the greatest Rabbis of his time.  At the age of twenty eight he was already serving as A.B.D. Frankurt-on-Oder and the Province of Schlesin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time before 1782, becoming disenchanted with the "antiquated Rabbinical authority" he retired from the Rabbinate, and settled in Berlin where he joined the Haskala Movement. The members were admirers of Moses Mendelssohn. Unl
2 CONC ike his father he supported Naftali H. Wessely at a time when he was most violently opposed by the most eminent German Rabbis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He wrote a satire, Ketav Yosher, published after his death in 1794, in which he sharply criticized the educational methods, superstitions and customs which had spread among the people during his life time. It was also meant as an 
2 CONC attempt to have Wessely's Divrei Shalom ve-Emet become Rabbinically accepted.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1784 he traveled to Italy, ostensibly to seek a cure for his rheumatism, but conceivably to meet the Wessely Rabbinical supporters. In Italy he wrote a pamphlet against R. Chaim Joseph David Azulai's Birkat Yosef, which appeare
2 CONC d in Leghorn in 1772.
2 CONT
2 CONT Becoming interested in manuscripts, R. Berlin edited Or Zaruah written by R. Isaac son of Moses of Vienna, which appeared in 1862. He also wrote [http://hebrewbooks.org/44948 Mitzpeh Yokta'el] published in Berlin in 1789, where he
2 CONC  used a pseudonym of Obadiah, son of Baruch Ish Polonia (of Poland). This contained a criticism on the Torat Yekutiel by R. Raphael son of R. Jekutiel Susskind Kohen, A.B.D. Altona-Hamburg-Wandsbeck. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The book caused a great stir among the Rabbis, including Berlin's father, who placed a ban on it and on the author.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father discovered the identity of its author, he tried to protect his son. Before this storm subsided, R. Saul Berlin published another controversial book, [http://hebrewbooks.org/1128 Besamim Rosh], in Berlin in 1793, to
2 CONC gether with comments entitled Kassa deHarsna. Although he claimed that he had only added notes to a manuscript by a certain R. Asher, son of Jehiel, R. Saul was strongly doubted. Because of its strong leniencies he was labelled as
2 CONC  an atheist.
2 CONT
2 CONT After wandering from country to country R. Saul went to London in 1794 to become their Ashkenazi Chief Rabbi, then being about fifty four years old. However, he died before entering office on November 16th (23rd of Chesvan) 1794 i
2 CONC n London.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was Sarah, daughter of R. Isaac Teomim) (see chapter 1V - Kitvei HaGeonim)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Source=
2 CONT
2 CONT The Unbroken Chain by Neil Rosenstein (1990) Volume 11, G 9.1 page 822-823)
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>><nowiki>------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saul_Berlin  '''Saul Berlin] (also Saul Hirschel after his father, born at Glogau 1740 – died in London November 16, 1794) was a German Jewish scholar who published a number of works in opposition to 
2 CONC rabbinic Judaism.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received his general education principally from his father, Hirschel Levin, who had served as rabbi of the Great Synagogue of London and as chief rabbi of Berlin. Saul, the eldest son, was given an education in both the Talmud 
2 CONC and secular subjects. His brother, Solomon Hirschell, eventually became Chief Rabbi of Great Britain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saul Berlin was ordained as a rabbi at 20. By 1768, aged 28, he had a rabbinic post in Frankfort-on-the-Oder in the Prussian province of Brandenburg. He married Sarah, the daughter of Rabbi Joseph Jonas Fraenkel of Breslau.
2 CONT In Berlin and Breslau (where he frequently went to visit his father-in-law, R. Joseph Jonas Fränkel), he came into personal contact with the representatives of the Jewish Enlightenment, and became one of its most enthusiastic adhe
2 CONC rents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berlin began his literary career with an anonymous circular letter, "Ketav Yosher" (An Epistle of Justice) (printed in Berlin, 1794, after the death of the author), which Hartwig Wessely warmly defended in his own contention with 
2 CONC the rabbis while pleading for German education among the Jews. Berlin used humor to describe what he viewed as the absurd methods of the Jewish schools, and alleges how the rabbinic casuistry—which then constituted the greater par
2 CONC t of the curriculum—injures the sound common sense of the pupils and deadens their noblest aspirations.
2 CONT
2 CONT He later wrote the pseudonymous work, "Mitzpeh Yekutiel" (The Watch-Tower of Yekutiel) (published by David Friedländer and his brother-in-law Itzig, Berlin, 1789), a polemic against the "Torat Yekutiel" of Raphael Kohen. The latte
2 CONC r, one of the most zealous advocates of rabbinic piety, was a rival candidate with Levin for the Berlin rabbinate, which induced Levin's son to represent ha-Kohen as a forbidding example of rabbinism.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the name "Ovadiah b. Baruch of Poland," Berlin attempted in this work to ridicule Talmudic casuistry,  and to stigmatize one of its foremost exponents not only as ignorant, but also as dishonest. The publishers declared in t
2 CONC he preface that they had received the work from a traveling Polish Talmudist, and had considered it their duty to print it and submit it to the judgment of specialists. To secure the anonymity more thoroughly, Berlin and his fathe
2 CONC r were named among those who were to pass upon it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berlin's statements, especially his personal attacks against those he disagreed with, undermined his cause. When it[clarification needed] reached Altona and Hamburg, where Raphael was chief rabbi, the work and its author was place
2 CONC d under the ban. The dispute that then arose concerning the validity of the ban turned entirely on the question of whether a personal element, like the attack upon the rabbi of Altona, justified such a punishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ezekiel Landau, chief rabbi of Prague and a near relation of Berlin, along with some other Polish rabbis supported the ban, while some declared the ban invalid. Even the former censured Berlin's actions after circumstances forced 
2 CONC him to acknowledge authorship.
2 CONT ￼
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Besamim Rosh, Berlin 1793
2 CONT Before the excitement over this affair had subsided, Berlin created a new sensation by another work. In 1793 he published in Berlin, under the title "Besamim Rosh" (Incense of Spices), 392 responsa purporting to be by Asher ben Je
2 CONC hiel, with many glosses and comments that he called "Kassa de-Harsna" (Fish Fare). Berlin says, for instance, ( in No. 257) that an insight into the principles of the Torah and its commands can not be gained directly from it or fr
2 CONC om tradition, but only by means of the philosophico-logical training derived from non-Jewish sources. However, Asher ben Jehiel had condemned the study of philosophy and even of the natural sciences as being un-Jewish and pernicio
2 CONC us (compare No. 58 of Asher's genuine responsa). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Besamim Rosh ascribes the following opinions are ascribed to the neo-Talmudists of the thirteenth century: "Articles of faith [creed] must be adapted to the times; and at present the most essential article is that we all are utter
2 CONC ly worthless and depraved, and that our only duty consists in loving truth and peace and learning to know God and His works" (l.c.). R. Asher is also alleged to be the author of the two responsa concerning the modification of the 
2 CONC ceremonial laws, especially of such as were burdensome to the Berlin youth.[clarification needed] Thus, for instance, it should be permitted to shave (No. 18), to drink non-kosher wine, "yayin nesek" (No. 36), and to ride on Shabb
2 CONC at. Berlin aroused a storm of indignation by thus fraudulently using the name of one of the most famous rabbis of the Middle Ages to combat rabbinism.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mordecai Benet first attempted to prevent the printing of the book in Austria, and then argued deception in a circular letter addressed to Berlin's father, by critically analyzing the responsa and arguing that they were spurious. 
2 CONC Levin tried in vain to defend his son. Berlin resigned his rabbinate and, to end the dispute, went to London where he died a few months later. In a letter found in his pocket, he warned everybody against looking into his papers, r
2 CONC equesting that they be sent to his father. He expressed the wish to be buried not in a cemetery, but in some lonely spot, and in the same garments in which he died. The exact historicity of Besamin Rosh is still disputed, with it 
2 CONC being unclear which parts are forgeries.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Character
2 CONT To understand this unique personality, note that, as a modern historian remarks[citation needed], that Berlin was a focal point for the rays of a sinking and rising period in Jewish history. Being a great Talmudist, he knew better
2 CONC  how to attack rabbinism, and was filled with a burning desire to lead his people toward intellectual freedom. Mendelssohn's and Wessely's timid attempts to inaugurate a new era did not appeal to him. With his youthful ardor he co
2 CONC uld not understand that the development of the popular consciousness is a slow process. An open championship of his ideas, however, would have meant a breach with father, wife, and children—in short, with all his associates; it be
2 CONC ing after all doubtful whether his sacrifices would have helped his cause. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His anonymous and pseudonymous authorship was a measure of policy and not of cowardice. He could not, however, escape the consequences of such a mode of warfare. It is debasing and embittering to attack secretly those whom one is 
2 CONC forced to praise in public. Hence Berlin became personal in his polemics, and nervous and dissatisfied with himself and the world because he knew he was misunderstood through his own fault[citation needed].
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides the works mentioned above, Berlin is said to have written a large number of rabbinic works, including notes to the whole Talmud[citation needed].
2 CONT ==Jewish Encyclopedia bibliography
2 CONT *	•	Azulai, Shem ha-Gedolim, ed. Wilna, ii. 20, 21;
2 CONT * 	•	Benet, in Literaturblatt des Orients, v. 53-55, 140-141 (fragment of his above-mentioned letter to Levin);
2 CONT * 	•	Brann, in the Grätz Jubelschrift, 1887, pp. 255–257;
2 CONT * 	•	Carmoly, Ha-'Orebim u-Bene Yonah, pp. 39–41;
2 CONT * 	•	Chajes, Minḥat Kenaot, pp. 14, 21;
2 CONT * 	•	Grätz, Gesch. der Juden, xi. 89, 151-153;
2 CONT * 	•	Horwitz, in Kebod ha-Lebanon, x., part 4, pp. 2–9;
2 CONT * 	•	Jost, Gesch. des Judenthums und Seiner Sekten, iii. 396-400 (curiously enough a defense of the authenticity of the responsa collection Besamim Rosh);
2 CONT * 	•	Landshuth, Toledot Anshe ha-Shem, pp. 84–106, 109;
2 CONT * 	•	M. Straschun, in Fuenn, Kiryah Neemanah, pp. 295–298;
2 CONT * 	•	Zunz, Ritus, pp. 226–228, who thinks that Isaac Satanow had a part in the fabrication of the responsa.L. G
