2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________
2 CONT From Meir Koppekmann's memoirs:
2 CONT
2 CONT Concerning my maternal ancestors, I have had the possibility to trace our family tree
2 CONT to the end of the 15th century. By a fortuitous circumstance, I obtained this material from a
2 CONT grandchild of a cousin of my late grandmother, Gnesja Zetlin, nee Landau. It had been
2 CONT collected by a Hermann Landau, who died in 1885. With the help of various Jewish
2 CONT encyclopedias, I was able to complete this information, so that the above-mentioned date of
2 CONT the origin of our family, that is late 15th century, is unquestionable, and evidence attests to the
2 CONT connection with the Family Meheran. In turn, the family tree of the Meherans, according to
2 CONT oral tradition, is said to reach back to the time of King David. This last bit, however, I leave
2 CONT to faith.
2 CONT It is interesting to note that all our ancestors, most of whom were rabbis, were
2 CONT Missnagim, and stood far from the Chassidic movement. The origin of the Family Meheran
2 CONT in Padua, shows that the family originally was Sephardic and only later went to the East.
2 CONT Around the year 1600, one finds them in Poland, where they produced a line of outstanding
2 CONT Kopelman/MY ANCESTORS 21
2 CONT rabbis. One of our ancestors whose identity can be authenticated was Rabbi Efraim Hirsch,
2 CONT Rabbi of Brest-Litovsk. The name Hirsch in no way denotes a family name, those did not
2 CONT exist at that time. It can perhaps be explained by the fact that a seal ring belonging to
2 CONT Salomon Hirschel, The Chief Rabbi of London, who was my grandmother’s grandfather,
2 CONT showed a shield framed by two deer rearing on their hind legs. This ring, which I still
2 CONT remember on the hand of my grandmother is unfortunately lost.
2 CONT The son of Efraim Hirsch was Rabbi Saul. He was rabbi in Lublin. His son,
2 CONT Yehoshuah, was first rabbi in the same town and later in Nikeburg. This shows a move
2 CONT westward, but not too far. He died in Krakow in 1663.
2 CONT His son was the learned Rabbi Saul. As a young man, he was rabbi in Rschesche,
2 CONT Poland, then Country Rabbi in Krakow and then received as the first one of our ancestors, a
2 CONT call from abroad, a call to Amsterdam. Unfortunately, he died on the journey to assume his
2 CONT new position and was buried 1707 in Glogau. His son was Arie Loeb, born 1690 in BrestLitovsk. Around 1730 he became a rabbi in Rschschov, then in Tarnopol and later in
2 CONT Glogau, where he pronounced the ban on Moshe Chaim Luzzate. He married the daughter of
2 CONT the famous Chacham Zwi. In 1740 he was called to Amsterdam, as his grandfather had been.
2 CONT He there built the large Lehrhaus, Study House.
2 CONT Arie Loeb was a center point in the great “Amulet Battle,” that was raging there at the
2 CONT time. His brother-in-law, Jacob Emden, a well-known rabbi in Altona, Denmark, had
2 CONT accused the equally well-kown Rabbi Eibenschutz of selling amulets bearing Kabbalistic
2 CONT signs, which smacked of the false teachings of Sabbatei. This quarrel divided the rabbis of
2 CONT the time in two irreconcilable camps, to the extent that the Danish Kaiser had to interfere.
2 CONT Loeb was in the camp of his brother-in-law, whose funeral oration at Loeb’s death is
2 CONT particularly well-known.
2 CONT His son was Zevi Hirschel Loebel, a.k.a Hart Lyon, the great-grandfather of my
2 CONT grandmother, also known as Hirsch Lewin, born in 1721. The name Lewin appears to
2 CONT already be a family name, but it was not inherited and so was lost. Already at the age of 36,
2 CONT on the recommendation of his Uncle Jacob Emden, he became Rabbi of Askenazi Jews at the
2 CONT great Synagogue in London. In 1764 he went to Halberstadt where he remained 6 years and
2 CONT then spent two years in Mannheim. In 1772, he was called to Berlin, where he officiated for
2 CONT twenty-eight years. He was the last Landesrabbiner and is known as the Berliner Rov. He
2 CONT Kopelman/MY ANCESTORS 22
2 CONT used to say, jokingly: that “. . in London I had money, but no Jews; in Mannheim I had Jews
2 CONT but no money; in Berlin I had neither Jews nor money.”
2 CONT Like his father, he too, took the part of Emden in the great quarrel. A friend of Moses
2 CONT Mendelson, it was Hirschel Loeb who granted Mendelson the approbation to translate the
2 CONT bible into German, later praising the resulting volume as “God pleasing.” He was also
2 CONT tolerant toward the Haskala, the Enlightenment Movement, but he came out very sharply
2 CONT against the assimilationist writing of Wessely. This last earned him the enmity of Berlin’s
2 CONT assimilated merchant families, such as that of Itzig Friedlander. Hirschel Loeb died in Berlin
2 CONT in 1801 and is buried together with his wife in the old Berlin cemetery in the Hamburger
2 CONT Strasse.
2 CONT Hirschel Loeb was a man of great erudition and sharp intelligence, and left a great
2 CONT deal of writing, only a small part of which was ever published. He was an avid book
2 CONT collector and had a notable library, which later was augmented by his son and in 1845 was
2 CONT bought and presented to the Great Synagogue Beth Hamidrash in London.
2 CONT (This library was sold and dispersed at Christie’s, London, 06/23/1999. GK has
2 CONT catalogue and also excerpts from TLS (London Times Literary Supplement) articles by H.
2 CONT Rabinovicz, discussing and strongly disapproving of the sale. The first is entitled “Selling
2 CONT the Family Silver,” the second “Selling the Heritage.)
2 CONT Kopelman/MY ANCESTORS 23
2 CONT
2 CONT RABBI SALOMON HIRSCHEL, CHIEF RABBI OF LONDON
2 CONT His son was Salomon Hirschel, the grandfather of my grandmother. He was born
2 CONT 1761 in London, while his father was rabbi there. Since his father left London soon
2 CONT thereafter, Salomon Hirschel received his education in Poland and Germany. He was first
2 CONT rabbi in Prenzlau and was then appointed to the same post in London that his father had held.
2 CONT He acted as Chief Rabbi of London for forty years. His wife was a Rebekka Lippmann from
2 CONT Koenigsberg. He died in London and was buried there in 1842. Salomon Hirschel entered
2 CONT the history of English Jewry as the “Londoner Rov” and a portrait of him hangs in the
2 CONT London Synagogue. I know the painting from an etching in the house of my mother, where
2 CONT he is depicted in the rabbinical costume of the time. (See etching above) He did not continue
2 CONT his father’s name of Lewin. As so many Jews who are surnamed after cities, some of his
2 CONT ancestors carry the name of London.
2 CONT One of Salomon Hirschel’s daughters married into the house of the famous Goa,
2 CONT Jecheskiel Landau, Rabbi of Prague. But about him, you can learn in any Jewish
2 CONT encyclopedia. A story by Max Brod, Der Bustag, “The Day of Repentence,” from his book
2 CONT “Jewish Feasts-Jewish Customs,” deals with a pilgrimage to his grave in Prague. He was
2 CONT misnagid, but was also honored by Chassidim.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
