1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://wikivisually.com/wiki/Sholom_Rokeach
2 CONT
2 CONT Sholom Rokeach (1781[2] – 10 September 1855), also known as the Sar Sholom (Hebrew: שר שלום‎‎, "Angel of Peace"[3]), was the first Belzer Rebbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Belzer Hasidim, he is known as "Der Ershter Rov" (the first rabbi), but in the city of Belz itself he was called "Der Alter Rov" (the old rabbi) in deference to the Bach, who presided as rabbi of Belz in the sixteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography His father was Rabbi Eleazar Rokeach, one of the sages of the Kloyz of Brody. The latter was the grandson of Rabbi Eleazar, author of Maaseh Rokeach, rabbi of Brody until 1736, then Chief Rabbi of Amsterdam.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was Rebbetzin Rivka Henna Ramraz.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Rabbi Eleazar died at the age of 32, Rivka Henna lived in Brody with her five orphaned children. She sent her son Sholom, around 11 years old at the time, to be raised by her brother, Rabbi Yissachar Dov Ramraz, rabbi of Sko
2 CONC hl, then in Galicia. Later on, he married Rabbi Yissachar Dov's daughter, Malka (1780 – 23 August 1853). They had five sons and two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the town of Skohl he was influenced by Rabbi Shlomo (Flam), the Rebbe of Skohl (also known as Reb Shlomo Lutzker), who was the personal writer and right-hand man of Rabbi Dov Ber of Mezeritch, the successor to the Baal Shem Tov
2 CONC , founder of Hasidism. Since his uncle (and father-in-law) was opposed to Hasidism, Rokeach would secretly be let down the window by his wife, to learn at Rabbi Shlomo Lutzker's beis midrash at night. He was also a disciple of the
2 CONC  Seer of Lublin.
2 CONT
2 CONT He composed several songs, most of them still sung by the Belzer Hasidim, including one niggun (melody) to Tzur Mishelo sung during the Shalosh Seudot (third Shabbat meal).
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of his teachings are preserved in an anthology entitled Midbar Kodesh.
2 CONT
2 CONT He reigned as Rebbe from 1817 (when he became rabbi in Belz) until his death in 1855. In addition to leading his Hasidim, he defended the beleaguered Jews of his district to the governor. In a famous exchange, the governor of the 
2 CONC district invited him to his office and said, "Do you know that I am the second Haman?" The Sar Sholom replied, "Luck was not on the side of the first one, either". The governor was so impressed by the Rebbe's firm stand that he pr
2 CONC omised to put an end to the Jewish persecution.
2 CONT
2 CONT He built a four-story synagogue in Belz which was inaugurated in 1843. It had a capacity of 5,000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it was uncommon in the early Hasidic movement for a son to succeed his father as Rebbe (typically the Rebbe or leader would be succeeded by a disciple), the Sar Shalom wished to be succeeded by the youngest of his five so
2 CONC ns, Yehoshua.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebbes of Belz Rabbi Sholom Rokeach (1781–1855) Rabbi Yehoshua Rokeach (1825–1894) Rabbi Yissachar Dov Rokeach (1854–1926) Rabbi Aharon Rokeach (1877–1957) Rabbi Yissachar Dov Rokeach (b. 1948)
2 CONT
