1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jacob Kahana is both the son-in law of R. Yissachar Ber (Klatzki) of Vilna, (the brother of the Gaon of Vilna) and the grandson of the Chief Rabbi of Amsterdam, Rabbi Saul Lowenstam (Loewenstamm).   
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Source: Jewish Encyclopedia 1906
2 CONT
2 CONT By: Solomon Schechter, N. T. London
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbinical author; died in Wilna 1826. His father was rabbi at Brestowitz, government of Grodno. Jacob was the son-in-law of R. Issachar of Wilna, brother of Elijah Wilna. He lived with his father-in-law, and was supported by him 
2 CONC for many years, so that he was able to devote his time to the study of the Law; and he became one of the leading Talmudical scholars in Wilna. After Issachar's death Jacob was appointed trustee of the charities of the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob was the author of "Shiṭṭot," a commentary on the tractate 'Erubin. The work is divided into three parts, the first consisting of novellæ on the Gemara, the second of novellæ on the Tosefta, and the third of novellæ on the co
2 CONC rresponding tractates in the Yerushalmi. The manuscript was revised and the work published in Lemberg, 1863, by Raphael Nathan Rabbinowicz. Bibliography:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Fuenn, Keneset Yisrael, p. 550;
2 CONT     idem, Ḳiryah Ne'emanah, p. 240.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Source:  Eliyahu's Branches:  The Descendants of the Vilna Gaon and His Family By Chaim Freedman P. 442-443 & 450
2 CONT
2 CONT "Yissakhar Ber adopted the surname Klatzki after his wife's uncle Yaakov Peretz Klatzki.  In a fascinating letter written by Yissakhar Ber to his mekhutan, Rabbi Shaul of Amsterdam, he urges Rabbi Shaul to send his grandson Rabbi 
2 CONC Yaakov Kahana, to Vilna as soon as possible in order that he marry Yissakhar Ber's daughter.   The reason given was that the level of scholarship was higher in Vilna as compared to Amsterdam.   A further comment in the same letter
2 CONC  requesting that Rabbi Shaul send certain books to Rabbi Yissakhar Ber indicates the physical isolation of Eastern European Jewry."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Brought up by his maternal grandfather Rabbi Shaul ABD Amsterdam.  After his marriage learned with his wife's uncle, the Gaon of Vilna.   He was so modest that he would not let his name be published in the list of the Gaon's stud
2 CONC ents.  Impoverished by the death of his father-in-law until given a communal appointment.   Refused an offer to succeed his grandfather in Amsterdam.   Nearly all of his scholarly writings have been lost, except "Gaon Yaakov." Bur
2 CONC ied next to Eliyahu Khassid, the Gaon's great-grandfather."  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.   The Unbroken Chain, Neil Rosenstein
2 CONT
2 CONT "R. Jacob Kahana of Vilna, died in 1826, the author of Gaon Yaakov, a commentary on the Talmud, published by R. Rafael Nathan Rabinowitz, Lvov, 1863, and arranged the work Eliyahu Rabba by R. Elijah the Gaon of Vilna, whose niece.
2 CONC .."
2 CONT
2 CONT 4..  Source - Zchus Avos Yogen Aleinu: http://zchusavos.blogspot.com/2011_02_01_archive.html
2 CONT
2 CONT "14 Adar - R' Yaakov ben R' Avrohom Kahana (1826), author of Gaon Yaakov"
