1 NOTE {geni:about_me} G11.3  R. Moses Katzenellenbogen, ABD Kremenchuk, Ukraine, where his only daughter was born about 1825.  He had been a student in the Volozhin Yeshiva under its founder, R. Haim of Volozhin and later settled in Fra
2 CONC nkfurt-on-Main.  He published, among other works, ''Divrei Moshe'', in Hamburg in 1851, responsa in 1857, ''Alpha Beta'', an alphabetical key to the 613 laws of the Torah with sayings of the earlier sages with his own commentary, 
2 CONC in two volumes in Frankfurt-on-Main in 1856-1857....and ''HaTorah VeHaMitzva'' on the 613 Jewish Laws (published in Stettin, no date).  In ''Divrei Moshe'' he writes in an introduction....:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...''after I was exiled from my birthplace, a place of Torah and Divine service, I travelled to a number of lands, Germany, Holland, and England but did not find peace to sustain my soul nor my family, where upon I was obligated t
2 CONC o spend several years in America.  During this time period I saw many thousands of people who had rejected, from upon themselves, the heavenly yoke of the oral Torah, the Talmud [the Oral Law] and the Posekim [the rabbinical decis
2 CONC ors] stating they did believed only that which was written in the Torah [Five Books of Moses].  And I saw that some of them were knowledgeable people having good qualities, upright, charitable and doing good deeds.  They had no ba
2 CONC d traits such as slander or talebearing, anger nor pride.  And I said I would greet them with peace.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source
2 CONT
2 CONT The Unbroken Chain - Neil Rosenstein (3rd Edition) Volume 3, Chapter 7, page 385
