2 CONT Frances Robbin Gewirz, 97, a lifelong Washington area resident and volunteer who was active in civic, cultural and Jewish groups, died of pneumonia Feb. 13 at her home in Washington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Gewirz, who was born in Washington, was a graduate of Central High School. She attended George Washington University, where she helped organize its Phi Sigma Sigma sorority chapter. She worked briefly for the U.S. Post Office
2 CONC  in the 1920s and was head of the USO program at the Jewish Community Center during World War II.
2 CONT She was a past chapter president of Hadassah, the National Council of Jewish Women, and B'nai B'rith Women. She also was a past president of the Adas Israel sisterhood and the Rock Creek Garden Club.
2 CONT Mrs. Gewirz also had served on the women's board of the National Symphony Orchestra and on the boards of the Washington Opera Guild and the Child Guidance Clinic. She and her husband, Morris, who died in 1973, founded the GWU Hill
2 CONC el Foundation.
2 CONT In addition to the District, she also maintained homes in Palm Beach, Fla., and Newport, R.I.
2 CONT Survivors include two sons, Bernard S., of Washington, and Carl S., of Bethesda; a daughter, Norma G. Kline of Washington; seven grandchildren; and five great-grandchildren.
