1 NOTE {geni:about_me} R’ Moises Tauber was a twin.  He was named in honor of two great-grandfathers, Moises Tauber (1725-1790) and Rabbi Moises Knopfelmacher (1718-1798).  Moises Tauber remained in Holesov and was still alive when the 1
2 CONC 880 census was taken.  He died in Holesov in 1882, age 79.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a younger son, Moises Tauber was not allowed to marry under the 1726 Familiants Law.  About 1833, he and Rachel Husserl, b. 1813, were united in a Jewish ceremony, but not legally married.  They lived together as husband and wi
2 CONC fe for over 30 years before — following the relaxation of the restrictions — they were formally married on or about 27 Dec 1865.  All of their children were born illegitimate and their births were listed under their mother’s surna
2 CONC me of Husserl.  Between, 27 Dec 1865 and early 1866, however, Moses Tauber acknowledged their four surviving children (Sara, Abraham, Amalia, and Michael), whereupon they were all declared legitimate.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachel Husserl, aka Rosalia Husserl, was born in Holesov, 1813, the daughter of Gabriel Husserl (1755-1817) and his wife, Bluma-Sara (Fischer) Husserl (1769-1841).   She d. in Holesov, 29 Sep 1885, age 72.  פנ באשה הצנועה מרת רחל 
2 CONC אשת כה משה טויבער זל מתה ב*דחהמ סכות תרמז לק תנצבה  “The humble woman, the deceased Mrs. Rachel, the wife of Rav Moshe Tauber.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Holesov, 1857:  TAUBER, Moses, b. 1804, [living with] HUSSERL, Rosi, b. 1815, son Abraham, b. 1844, son Michael, b. 1853, dau Betti, b. 1841, and dau Mali, b. 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT Holesov, 1869:  [pg. 58] TAUBER, Moses, b. 1804; wife Rosi, b. 1814; son Abraham, b. 1844; dau Amalia, b. 1850; and son Michael, b. 1852 [with insert attached showing that Michael Tauber, son of Moses Tauber and Rosi geb. Husserl,
2 CONC  was b. 3 Aug 1852].
2 CONT
2 CONT Holesov, 1880:  [pg. 61] TAUBER, Moses, b. 1803; wife Raichl, b. 1813; and dau Mali, b. 1851.
