1 NOTE {geni:about_me} EPHRAIM BEN JACOB HA-KOHEN: on [https://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/5799-ephraim-ben-jacob-ha-kohen Jewish Encyclopedia]
2 CONT By: Louis Ginzberg, A. Peiginsky
2 CONT Lithuanian Talmudist; born at Wilna 1616; died June 3, 1678, at Ofen, Hungary. Driven by the Chmielnicki persecutions from his native city, where he was dayyan, he went to Moravia. He filled the office of rabbi, first at Trebitsch
2 CONC  and then at Ofen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ephraim's works include: "Sha'ar Efrayim," responsa arranged according to the order of the Shulḥan 'Aruk (Sulzbach, 1688); and "Maḥaneh Efrayim," notes to the Pentateuch, which he left in manuscript.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography:
2 CONT Sha'ar Efrayim, Introduction;
2 CONT Fuenn, Kiryah Ne'emanah, p. 73, who wrongly calls Ephraim's father Aaron instead of Jacob;
2 CONT Emden, Megillat Sefer, Index, Warsaw, 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Regularly fasted all week long from Shabbat till Shabbat Source: Megillat Sefer by Rabbi Yaakov Emeden (his grandson) (see Documents in Media)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Author of Sha'ar Ephraim
2 CONT
2 CONT Either grandson of Rabbi Elijah Ba'al Shem of Chelm or married to a grandaughter of Rabbi Elijah Ba'al Shem of Chelm
2 CONT
2 CONT Ephraim ha-Kohen, then rabbi at Alt-Ofen (now Budapest),  Yaakov Ashkenazy was married to Nechamah, the daughter of Rabbi Efraim Hakohen of Vilna, the author of “Shaarei Efraim”.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
