1 OCCU AVAD Lakenbach, rabi of Lackenbach in Hungary, אב"ד לאקענבאך
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Notes by Elisha Koppel below: Also by PMS
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://kehilalinks.jewishgen.org/Lackenbach/html/Rabbis_of_lackenbach.html  '''Rabbi Benjamin ASCH], the 6th son of MaHaRaM A"SH. Came to Lackenbach in 1742 (תק"ב Taf Kof Beit) from Ungarisch Brod; was also Rabbi of  Kobersdorf. 
2 CONC           More information about him, in:   Bernhard Wachstein, Die Grabinschriften des alten Juden-friedhofes in Eisenstadt, Wien: Holzhausen 1922, pp. 78-83;          "The sixth child of Meir b. Isaac was Benjamin [OR 3666]. As 
2 CONC rabbi of Lackenbach in Hungary, he signed the approbation for Meorei A"Sh on Thursday, 22 Cheshvan [5]526 [6 November 1765, a Wednesday!]. In the same work, p. 88c, Kallir mentions an explanation of a Talmud passage in his name. B
2 CONC efore his term in Lackenbach, we find him among the leaders of the community in Ungarisch-Brod between 1728 and 1747 [80/3].
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Benjamin's sons was Salomon; he was a learned man but - judging from the titles he is given [80/4] - not a rabbi [d]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His son-in-law Jacob of Schlaining near Rechnitz in Hungary was a student of his great-uncle Eleazar Kallir, whose Chavat Yair heChadash he edited (Prague 1792) in order to improve his financial situation [80/4].
2 CONT
2 CONT [80/3] Frankl-Gruen, Geschichte der Juden in Ungarisch-Brod, p. 23.
2 CONT
2 CONT [80/4] [Hebrew quotation from preface to Chavat Yair heChadash not translated; continuation on p. 81:] On the title page, he is also mentioned as editor. At the beginning there is Eize Divrei Tora. K. himself did not try to profit
2 CONC  from his work as an author. On the contrary, he offered his manuscripts for print free of charge and was even willing to order books worth 100 ducats; cf. the announcement in the above-mentioned work.
2 CONT
2 CONT [d] According to Otzar haRabbanim, he became rabbi in Lackenbach in 1770 [OR 18740]. [http://kehilalinks.jewishgen.org/Lackenbach/html/Rabbis_of_lackenbach.html  ''Source]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elisha Koppel states otherwise:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Sefer כתנות אור by the Panim Merios it is written in the Haskomah as follows that R binyomin Asch was the eldest son of The Panim meirot. The Ponim Meirot was born in 1670 so it can be assumed that R Binyomin Asch was born 
2 CONC latest in the early 1690's. While the Yaavetz was born in 1697 so it is unlikely that he married his daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT What makes it impossible is that the Paniim Meirot quotes in his responsa that he was in Amsterdam for the wedding of his youngest child R Yitzchok to the daughter of the Chacham tzvi and he speaks about the family. He doesnt ment
2 CONC ion at all that he was already related to teh family as his eldst child married R yaacov Emdens daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT None of the historians bring this down and I found this at only one source which most probably mixed it up with his brother R Yizchok as they did not quote R Yitzchok being married to the chacham Tzvis gaughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Die Grabschriften des alten Judenfriedhofes in Eisenstadt- Dr. Bernhard Wachstein- page 80
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT While some claim he was the son-in-law of Rabbi Yaakov Emdin, ( Meor haChaim, 1960 p.22, Shut Le'Horos Nasson V1 p.15 ) others dispute that.
2 CONT
2 CONT As far as I was able to research I was not able to find proof that she was indeed the daughter of Rabbi Emdin (and found the basis of those claims not to be enough reliable to go along with it as of now). 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also intersting to note that the Arugas Habosem does quote the Chacham Tzvi many times, but never as his grandfather. When he mentions the Panim Meiros he does mention that it's his grandfather. MH:N21  MH:I100
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT PMS According to Avraham Benedikt in Beer Hamoadim p.402 that there's some difficulties saying that R Binyamin was for sure a son-in-law of the Yaavetz but there are a few serious sources confirming it, He says that  he heard from
2 CONC  Yitzchak Shiff from NY that he heard clearly from his grt grandfather Shmuel Zanvil Schiff (who was a expert in the genealogy) that R Binyamin did not have any children from his first wife so he married the SECOND time the dau of
2 CONC  the Yaavetz (that can help answer Elisha Koppel's Question about why the Panim Meirot didnt mention the Chacham Tzvi as his mechutan and make the years possible).
2 CONT
2 CONT Also that Yitzchak Shiff heard from Yehuda Hirschler who was a secretary and bookkeeper in Eisenstadt for 30 years that while there he searched the archives of Eisenstadt and Lackenbach and found there a letter that clearly titles
2 CONC  R Binyamin among other titles "son-in-law" of the Gaon Yaakov of Altona which is Reb Yakov Emdin indeed
