1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Max Koppelman's papers:
2 CONT
2 CONT This first wife of my grandfather’s, died, barely forty years of age, in 1858 (?)
2 CONT leaving Grandfather alone with a large family, including two still unmarried daughters. It
2 CONT was natural that a second marriage would have to be arranged—such a household without a
2 CONT woman in charge was unthinkable.
2 CONT A suitable candidate was soon found. In Lublin, in Poland, where my grandmother,
2 CONT then divorced, was living with her relatives. She had been married for ten years to the
2 CONT grandson of the famous Rabbi Jesheskiel Landau of Prague, but the marriage remained
2 CONT childless, which, according to the custom of the times, was the cause of the divorce. She,
2 CONT herself, was a Jachsanit, a prestigious person, the granddaughter of the Chief Rabbi of
2 CONT London, Salomon Hirschl. He was a friend of Moses Mendelson, and it was he who gave
2 CONT Mendelson the Hochscher to translate the Tanach into German. It is possible that my
2 CONT grandfather, knowing of her barren first marriage, speculated that she would not bear him any
2 CONT more children to add to his brood of eight, which undoubtedly was more than enough for
2 CONT him.
2 CONT In this he was soon to be disappointed. After their marriage, which took place in
2 CONT Mohilev, her birthplace, in 1859, she bore him two daughters. The elder, born in 1860,
2 CONT called Sheindl, (Eugenia) was my mother.
