2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestor of the Soloveitchik dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Soloveitchik family includes many significant rabbinical forebears, most notably Rabbi Chaim Volozhiner, famed Talmudist and founder of the Volozhin yeshiva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Chaim Volozhiner ( 1749-1821), was a student of the Vilna Gaon, and thus some students of Brisk talk of a line of tradition extending "from Moses at Sinai, to Joshua, to the Elders ... to the Vilna Gaon, to Rabbi Chaim Voloz
2 CONC hiner, and then to the Soloveitchik dynasty."
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaim_Soloveitchik (see bio)
2 CONT
2 CONT Most scholars, however, begin the Soloveitchik dynasty with Rabbi Joseph Dov (HaLevi) Soloveitchik known as the Beis HaLevi (see below), as he was the first rabbi of Brisk surnamed Soloveitchik. More significantly, the "Brisker st
2 CONC yle" described below can already be found to some degree in the Beis HaLevi's works, which is not the case for earlier ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yosef_Dov_Soloveitchik_(Beis_Halevi) (see bio)
2 CONT
2 CONT All members of the Soloveitchik family are descended from the tribe of Levi and thus sometimes go by the descriptor "HaLevi".
2 CONT
2 CONT The surname "Soloveitchik", in fact, is Polish for "nightingale"; it was chosen by the family because the primary duty of the Levites in the Temple in Jerusalem was singing. (Note that the surname "Soloveitchik" can be spelled eit
2 CONC her as presented, or as "Soloveichik", without the "t".
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wiki.geni.com/index.php/Jewish_Dynasties
2 CONT
