1 NAME Aryey Leib ABD Kremenets /Leib/
2 GIVN Aryey Leib ABD Kremenets
2 SURN Leib
2 NICK Leibush
1 SEX M
1 OCCU Rabbi; "Rav"
2 PLAC Kremenets', Ternopil', Ukraine
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1715
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [Alternative possibility for identity of Horenstein ancestor.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Served in the rabbinate of Kremenets for over 30 years and gave his approbation for many religious works.*
2 CONT
2 CONT 1739:  R' Arye Leyb served as the Volin representative at meetings of the Council of Four Lands for the purposes of dividing the tax burden.  He apparently was very rich, and the community owed him substantial amounts.*  That same
2 CONC  year, in Berestechko, he cosigned a document as a trustee of Volin.**
2 CONT
2 CONT 1740:  He received a Letter of Standing from King August III, in which the king places him under his aegis and guards his rights to the rabbinate of Kremenets, where he had been serving for quite a while. As was the case for any r
2 CONC abbi of a principal city, any revenues that came to the rabbi of Kremenets would belong to him, even if he left the city. The same decree includes his appointment as trustee of the district and the Four Lands, and a promise to app
2 CONC oint his son after him, if he is worthy of such. The king commands that the rabbi be paid all debts owed to him and places the responsibility for this on the heads of the community. The rabbi is also declared exempt from paying ta
2 CONC xes and from responsibility for the community's debts.*
2 CONT
2 CONT It can be assumed that this privilege was given in return for a substantial payment - and to the discomfort of the community, as rabbis were generally appointed for three years by the community leaders.*
2 CONT
2 CONT 1741: R' Arye Leyb, along with the rest of the leaders of the Four Lands, signed an approbation of the Amsterdam edition of the Talmud and, in 1752, of the responsum House of Abraham.*
2 CONT
2 CONT 1746:  Signed an approbation.*
2 CONT
2 CONT 1750:  R' Arye Leyb  acquired from King August III the privilege of serving as A"bd in Kremenets and its surroundings, and as district trustee as well as trustee of the House of Israel throughout the Four Lands.*  As treasurer, he
2 CONC  became responsible for tax payments to the King in all of Volyn.***
2 CONT
2 CONT 1758:  The municipal records of Kremenets record a protest by the Volin district trustees (the rabbi of Kremenets, R' Arye Leyb, and the rabbi of Ludmir, R' Shaul) against the communities of Ostra and Lutsk. The trustees claimed t
2 CONC hat the community of Ostra refused to pay the expenses, which approached 38,000 gold pieces, incurred by the district trustees while they attended to the Jews' affairs in that community. A District Council convened in Rachmanov to
2 CONC  deal with these expenses certified their justice, but the community of Ostra did not pay even the amount that it acknowledged.*  (from Documents and Inscriptions, vol. III, no. 2058; summarized in the Jewish Encyclopedia, 1906).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1762:  He may have moved away from the Kremenets area as, by that year, he was no longer listed as an official and he no longer signed documents.[Mazkeret li-Gedole Ostraha. Entry 127, p. 169]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1775:  Signed and dated his approbation for Michah Chadasha (A New Offering).* [Mazkeret li-Gedole Ostraha. Entry 127, p. 169]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was involved in the famous dispute between R' Yakov Emden and R' Yonatan Eybeshuts in Poland. [R' Yakov Emden, Torat ha-Kenaot, p. 64.]*
2 CONT
2 CONT He was probably a Dayan (judge) in Ostroh. [Ostraha: Megilat Polin; 1966, p. 49]
2 CONT
2 CONT * = Pinkas Kremenets
2 CONT ** = Haita Eiyera: Sefer Zikaron LeKehilot Berestetzka, p. 17
2 CONT ***= Kremenets: Yisgor Book
