2 CONT Additional Note of genealogical interest from the 3rd Edition of Neil Rosenstein's Unbroken Chain, Volume 3, page 338:
2 CONT
2 CONT R. Ezekiel also states in the 1720 edition of Halachot Rav Alfas (part one), published in Amsterdam, that he was descendant of R. Nathan Spira, died in 1577, ABD Grodno and author of  Mevo Shearim and Imrei Shefer (grandfather of 
2 CONC R. Nathan Nata Spira, ABD Cracow, born about 1584 and died in 1633, author of Megaleh Amtkot).  This would most likely be on his maternal side.
2 CONT
2 CONT Knesset Yechezkel: https://jewishmiscellanies.com/2023/11/26/shut-knesset-yechezkel-by-rabbi-yechezkel-katzenellenbogen-altona-1732/ 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT From geni.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 1668 or 1669, Ezekiel himself was uncertain of the fact. He grew up in Brest where he studied under R. Mordecai Susskind,son of R. Moses Rothenberg who lived there until about 1691.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was first A.B.D. of Zdzieciol (Zitel); then A.B.D. Raseiniai. In 1707 he was called to Keidaniai and to Altona in 1714 where he served the community for 35 years until his death. The community at this time was under Danish rule
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Ezekiel married Ester, daughter of R. Solomon Zalman, who was the son of R. Joel of Brest (the grandson of R. Joel Sirkes called the BaCh). Ester died in 1748 and was buried in the Koningstrasse Cemetry, Altone (Tombstone no. 3072
2 CONC ).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The Chief Rabbi (Av Beth Din) of Keidaniai (Lithuania), until 1714 was Rabbi (R’) Ezekiel Katzenellenpogen (a descendant through the female line who had adopted the name from the Katzenellenbogen male line.)[1] He then became AB"D
2 CONC  of the famous triple community of Altona-Hamburg-Wansdbeck where he remained until his death in 1749.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Altona July 9th (23rd Tammuz) 1749. Buried next to his wife. Tombstone 3073.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son R’ David succeeded him in 1714 as ABD Keidaniai, and he was succeeded by his son, R’ Meshullam Zalman AB"D Keidaniai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source
2 CONT
2 CONT Author of "Kenesset Yezekiel" Mayim Yechezkiel (published 1786) Tefillot le-Yartzheit (published 1727) Tzava'at Yechezkiel - His Ethical Will (published 1750, and again in 1871 by a descendant) Me'orer Zikkaron (published 1727) He
2 CONC  also wrote numerous approbations.
2 CONT
