2 CONT Emden, Megillat Sefer, pp 121-140, Warsaw 1897 Furst, Bibl, Jud.ii 179 Gratz, Gesch. x 375 Kohan (Kahana) in Ha-Shahar, 1874 p. 299 Steinschneider, Cat. Bodl
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT From Wikipedia.org:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ezekiel Katzenellenbogen ben Abraham (born in Lithuania about 1670; died at Altona, July 9, 1749) was a Polish-German rabbi.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first rabbi at Kėdainiai (Keidani), he was called to Altona in 1714. This call he owed to the efforts of Issachar Kohen, an influential member of the Altona congregation; and Katzenellenbogen in return secured the election of K
2 CONC ohen's son-in-law to the rabbinate of Keidani. Jacob Emden, who reports this story in his Megillat Sefer (pp. 121-140), seems, however, to have been prejudiced against Katzenellenbogen, whom he describes as a man of very low moral
2 CONC  character, an ignoramus, and a poor preacher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ezekiel ben Abraham Katzenellenbogen's descendants for four generations occupied rabbinates in various Polish communities. His epitaph is found in Blogg's Sefer ha-Ḥayyim (p. 337; Hanover, 1862). He wrote: Keneset Yeḥezḳel, respon
2 CONC sa, Altona, 1732; Tefillot le-Yarẓait, prayers and rituals for Jahrzeit, ib. 1727; Ẓawwa'at R. Yeḥezḳel, his will, Amsterdam, 1750; Mayim [Mi-Yam?] Yeḥezḳel, homilies on the Pentateuch, Porick, 1786; and Leḥem Yeḥezḳl, Talmudic no
2 CONC vellæ (mentioned in his preface to Keneset Yeḥezḳel, but never published). According to Steinschneider (Cat. Bodl.), Katzenellenbogen is probably the author of the Me'orer Zikkaron (Altona, 1727), an index to Talmudic passages wit
2 CONC h an abstract of the decisions of Rashi, the tosafists, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT References from the Jewish Encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Emden, Megillat Sefer, pp. 121-140, Warsaw, 1897; Julius Fürst, Bibl. Jud. ii.179; Heinrich Grätz, Gesch. x.375; Kohan (Kahana[disambiguation needed]), in Ha-Shaḥar, 1874, p. 299; Moritz Steinschneider, Cat. Bodl.; Walden, Shem ha
2 CONC -Gedolim he-Ḥadash, p. 29, Warsaw, 1864; Duckesz, lwwah le-Moshab, Cracow, 1903; Benjacob, Oẓar ha-Sefarim
2 CONT
