1 NAME Ellen Rebecca /Braude/
2 GIVN Ellen Rebecca
2 SURN Braude
2 _MARNM Rabinowitz
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Cape Town
3 STAE Western Cape
3 CTRY South Africa
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1892
2 ADDR
3 CITY Plungė
3 STAE Telšiai County
3 CTRY Lithuania
1 DEAT
2 DATE 27 MAY 1967
1 OCCU Shopkeeper
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ellen Rebecca Braude was the tall, beautiful, blue-eyed blonde daughter of Moses and Clara Braude of Mossel Bay, South Africa. The family emigrated from Tels, Russia, after the Russian authorities began making life
2 CONC  increasingly unbearable for Jews. She married Benjamin Louis Rabinowitz in the Worcester synagogue in 1913, eventually settling in the small fishing village of Port Nolloth, on the west coast of South Africa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the birth of her first child (Joseph Saul) in Porterville in December 1914, she returned to Port Nolloth in the cargo steamer, the SS Nautilus.  The boat was too big to get over the reef just outside the harbour, so to get t
2 CONC o the jetty, everything, including passengers, had to be transferred between boat and jetty by a tug. The passengers were place in deep baskets and lifted and lowered by crane onto the jetty, a very tricky business in rough seas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the Jewish New Year her husband would don his long jacket and top hat, which he wore on their wedding day. The table would be laid with fine silver cutlery and even in the desert, Ellen somehow managed to find flowers to adorn 
2 CONC the Passover table. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ellen spoke five languages (Yiddish, Russian, German, Afrikaans and English), and was fond of quoting poetry like “The Erf King” by Goethe. German, English and High Dutch books lined their bookshelves. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband Louis died suddenly in 1928, she was devastated and heart broken, but had to take over the running of the shop. Located at the edge of town, she’d walk to and from home four times a day through the thick sand. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After five of her six children had left Port Nolloth to continue their education school in Cape Town, she was left isolated in the back of beyond, carrying on the business with which she supported and educated a sizeable family in
2 CONC  the big city. Her thoughts were only for her children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1942, after two of her sons had been captured by the German army in Libya, Ellen was finally persuaded to sell up and leave Port Nolloth. All the goods were sold to Harold Gelb in Springbok. Her daughter Linkel remembered helpi
2 CONC ng her mother pack up, and finding her wedding shoes - white satin, pointed toed “winklepickers”. They settled in Sea Point, Cape Town, bringing their dog “Putti” with them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Having lived a hard, yet rich and adventurous life, she was blessed with grand children and great grandchildren, finally leaving the earthly plane at the ripe age of 82 years old, on May 27th, 1967. She is buried in Pinelands ceme
2 CONC tery, Cape Town. 
