0 @N6000000004339589542@ NOTE Rabbi Isaiah ben Avraham Ha-Levi Horowitz (The Shlah)
1 CONT (1565-1630)
1 CONT
1 CONT Rabbi Isaiah ben Avraham Ha-Levi Horowitz, known as the Shlah after the title of one of his major works Shnei Luchos Ha-Bris, was a renowned halachist, kabbalist and communal leader.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Prague he moved with his father to Poland in his youth, and after building a reputation as an exceptional scholar, he gained a number of positions as head of beis dins (religious courts) in the area. By 1606 he reached the
1 CONC  level of the head of the Frankfort on the Main beis din, one of the most influential of the period.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the Jews were expelled from there in 1614, he returned to his native Prague, where he remained as a rabbi. In 1621 at the death of his wife, he
1 CONT moved to Eretz Israel, remarried and settled in Jerusalem as one of the heads of the Ashkenazic community there. In 1625 he was captured by the Arab ruler, and ransomed for an exorbitant sum. The Shlah died in Tiberius and is buri
1 CONC ed near the  Rambam.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his life the Shlah was a wealthy and active philanthropist, supporting Torah learning especially in Jerusalem. In his many Kabbalistic, homiletic and halachic works, he stressed the joy in every action, and how one should c
1 CONC onvert the evil inclination into good, two concepts that impacted on Jewish thought through to the eighteenth-century, and greatly influenced the development of the
1 CONT Chassidic movement.
1 CONT
1 CONT FROM HOROWITZ FAMILY WEBSITE:
1 CONT
1 CONT Rabbi Yishayahu bem Avraham Ha-Levi Horowitz (The Holy ShLaH)
1 CONT R. Yishayhu ben Avraham Ha-Levi (about 1565, Prague - 1630, Tiberias), known
1 CONT under the name of "SheLaH Ha-Kadosh" (The Holy SheLaH, which is the initials
1 CONT of the title of his most famous book "Shnei Luchot Ha-Brit" -Two Tablets of the Covenant"), son of R. Avraham ben Shabtai Sheftel, author of "Emek Berakha" - prominent scholar, rabbi, kabbalist, a leader of his generation, one of 
1 CONC the great framers of Jewish ethics and morality in the modern era.
1 CONT
1 CONT Still a child he moved to Poland with his father who became his first teacher. His other teachers in Poland were R. Meir ben Gedalyahu (MaHaRaM) of Lublin, one of the leading Polish halakhic authorities, R. Shlomo ben Yehudah Leib
1 CONC  from Cracow, one of the most famous teachers in Polish yeshivot, and R. Yehoshua Falk Ha-Cohen, the author of the book "SaMa".
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Chaya, daughter of wealthy R. Avraham Maul from Vienna and
1 CONT apparently all his life he lived in favorable material conditions, and thus he could spend a considerable part of his income on charity and books which were very expensive in that period and affordable by just a few. He could also
1 CONC  afford himself to invite his numerous disciples to his table. Very soon he became one of the leaders of the Jewish community of Poland, known for his expert knowledge of Torah and Talmud. In 1590 he took part in one of the meetin
1 CONC gs of the Council of Four Lands in Lublin, and his signature appears under the decree condemning the purchase of rabbinic positions.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1597 R. Yishayahu published the book "Emek Berakha" written by his father
1 CONT and to which he added his own commentary under noticeable Kabbala influence.
1 CONT This work made the name of R. Yishayahu well known in the Jewish world, and
1 CONT he received offers to take high rabbinic positions in major Jewish communities.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1600 R. Yishayahu became A.B.D. of Dubno, in 1602 - A.B.D. and Head of
1 CONT the yeshiva in Ostraha (Volyn), in 1606 - A.B.D. in Frankfurt-am-Main. In 1614, together with other Jews, he was expelled from Frankfurt shortly before the Jewish quarter was plundered. He then returned to his native Prague. Here 
1 CONC he was a dayan, head of the yeshiva and shared the position of the rabbi of Prague with R. Shlomo Ephraim Luntshits (R. Shlomo ben Aharon of Leczyca, the author of "Keli Yaqar") until 1620 when his wife died. Soon after her death 
1 CONC R. Yishayahu left for Eretz Israel.
1 CONT
1 CONT In accordance with family tradition R. Yishayahu kept his intention to go to the Holy Land in secret until the last moment. He did so for he did not wish to confuse the whole community which would try to dissuade him from leaving.
1 CONT Only his eldest son knew about his plans and asked him to change his mind, but in vain: R. Yishayahu was determined to go to the Land of Israel, where he hoped to achieve two goals: to strengthen the Jewish community and to study 
1 CONC Kabbala which then played a central role in his philosophy. This is clearly discernible in his most important work, "Two Tablets of the Covenant" which he began to write in Prague shortly before his departure in 1621 and completed
1 CONC  in 1623 in Jerusalem.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first part of R. Yishayahu's route was to Frankfurt, he evidently wished to say good-bye to the community which was so important to him. From Frankfurt he travelled to Venice, and, finally, embarked by boat from Italy. In 22 d
1 CONC ays, on the day of Rosh Ha-Shana (Jewish New Year) the boat arrived in Tripoli where heavy fighting prevented him from disembarking. A group of armed men pursued him, but he managed to escape and set sail for Syria, where he went 
1 CONC ashore just before Yom Kippur. He was received with much warmth and honor by local Sephardi communities, which he modestly attributed to his father's notoriety, but which in fact was attributable to his own. In Damascus and Caleb 
1 CONC (Aleppo) he looked for books on Kabbala and, quite surprisingly, he found one in the house of R. Shmuel Vital, a son of famous Damascus Rabbi Chaim Vital. This was the manuscript of the latter's book "Ez Ha-Chaim" ("The Tree Of Th
1 CONC e Life").
1 CONT
1 CONT Two delegations came to R. Yishayahu - from Safed and Jerusalem, both of
1 CONT which asked him to be the Ashkenazi rabbi of their town. Despite the kabbalistic tradition in Safed, he preferred Jerusalem - "because of the great holiness of this city". Another reason for his decision could be that walled Jerus
1 CONC alem seemed to be much safer than Safed which was not surrounded by walls.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1621 R. Yishayahu arrived in Jerusalem, after visiting Safed "in order not to hurt its community" and pray at the graves of its great kabbalists. He almost immediately became Ashkenazi A.B.D of Jerusalem and shortly thereafter 
1 CONC remarried, evidently prompted by the kabbalistic belief of the time that an unmarried Jew had no right to live in Eretz Israel. He was also appointed the head of the Ashkenazi yeshiva, thus strengthening his leadership in the Ashk
1 CONC enazi community of Jerusalem. He enjoyed high esteem and respect with the city's Sefardi community as well. In 1625, together with 15 other Jewish scholars, rabbis and communal workers, he was imprisoned by the Pasha (Ibn Faruh) -
1 CONC  a local Muslim ruler appointed by the Turkish Sultan. They were held in prison for about two and a half weeks, starving and tortured, until an exorbitant sum of money had been paid as a ransom.
1 CONT
1 CONT R. Yishayahu came back to Jerusalem and very soon discovered that after the
1 CONT payment of the ransom both the Ashkenazi and Sefarfi communities found
1 CONT themselves deep in debt, and he tried to improve the situation with help from abroad. But it was clear that to stay in Jerusalem was very dangerous, and along with the other leaders of the Ashkenazi community, he fled to Safed.
1 CONT
1 CONT He lived his remained years in Safed and Tiberias, where he died and was
1 CONT buried by R Yohanan ben Zaccay and his disciples who put stones on the grave
1 CONT which lacked a matzevah (tombstone). R. Yishayahu's grave lies close to that
1 CONT of Ha-RaMBaM. His second wife, Chava, a daughter of R. Eleazer, and their
1 CONT daughter died soon after his death. From his first marriage he had two sons,
1 CONT R. Shabtai Sheftel and R. Yakov, and a daughter. R Yakov married Sarah, a
1 CONT daughter of the famous R. Eleazer, one of the prominent Cracow scholars, a
1 CONT son of R. Naftali Chayot, A.B.D. of Prague. R.Yakov began to publish his
1 CONT commentaries on his father's siddur "Shaar Ha-Shamaim", but he died before
1 CONT publication had been completed. In Cracow R. Yishayahu's daughter, Nechama,
1 CONT was married to R. Chaim Faivel, a son of famous R. David Zakharya, called
1 CONT "Mendelin".
1 CONT
1 CONT R. Yishayahu who descended from a well-to-do family and married into an even
1 CONT wealthier one, lived a comfortable life, not knowing what need or economic
1 CONT dependence is. In Eretz Israel, too, there was a servant in his spacious and well furnished apartment, which was allotted to him by the Ashkenazi community of Jerusalem. For his part he refused to receive a salary for his work and
1 CONC  spent much on philanthropy, particularly to support Torah scholars.
1 CONT
1 CONT R. Yishayahu made a great contribution to the development and strengthening
1 CONT of Ashkenazi communities in Eretz Israel. He lent a big sum of money to the
1 CONT community of Jerusalem which remained in debt to his daughter even after his
1 CONT death.
1 CONT
1 CONT R. Yishayahu came to Eretz Israel for the purpose of dedicating himself to studying Torah and fulfilling the mitzvoth connected with life in Eretz Israel. He was happy that he had been privileged to teach his fellow Jews Torah in 
1 CONC Eretz Israel, the Holy Land, and in Jerusalem, the Holy City, to instruct them and to guide them to love and fear the Lord, "faithfully and with all one's heart". He hoped and planned that thousands of Jews would come after him, a
1 CONC nd he himself would achieve great heights in Torah learning and administration of community life. He hoped for the revival of the Jerusalem Ashkenazi community under his leadership and as a result of his extensive contacts with As
1 CONC hkenazi communities in the Diaspora.
1 CONT
1 CONT By reason of R. Yishayahu's profound personal respect for eastern Sefardi
1 CONT communities, they influenced him greatly as a result of his encounter with their culture, customs and tradition. He was happy when he had an opportunity to pray with and provide a drasha in a sephardic congregation, "in the Holy L
1 CONC anguage, very clearly", and he adopted some of their liturgical traditions.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Eretz Israel the works of Ha-ARI (R. Yitzhak Luria), R. Moshe Cordovero and R. Yosef Karo made a strong impression on R. Yishayahu. He called them "three great holy ones....true angels of the Almighty". His study of the writing
1 CONC s of these scholars reinforced the kabbalistic elements in his works.  For him Kabbala was a teaching of the "sages of truth who have been initiated into the secrets of the Almighty passed in an unbreakable chain from man to man b
1 CONC eginning with Moshe Rabenu (Moses) on Mount Sinai".
1 CONT
1 CONT R. Yishayahu believed that the moment of revelation of the secret knowledge
1 CONT contained in the book "Zohar" as part of the preparations for the approaching geula (Salvation) had come: "this last generation is allowed to learn the Secret Teachings in public because they are close to geula and will make no er
1 CONC rors". In accordance with this, in his approach to Torah learning, the fulfillment of mitzvoth and questions of faith R. Yishayahu prefers RaMBaN's and other kabbalists' point of view as opposed to that of RaMBaM and other rationa
1 CONC lists.
1 CONT
1 CONT The best known of all R. Yishayahu's writings is his pioneering work "SheLaH" - "Two Tablets of the Covenant". The first edition was published in Amsterdam in 1649. Later this book was reissued at least 10 times. Some of these edi
1 CONC tions were made possible by his son - R. Shabtai Sheftel who added to it his preface - "Vavei Ha-Ammudim".
1 CONT
1 CONT Another important work by R. Yishayahu is his commentary to siddur "Sha'ar
1 CONT Ha-Shamaim" (The Gates of Heaven) which was published by his great grandson
1 CONT in 1717 also in Amsterdam. The title of the siddur originated from the words
1 CONT contained in parashat ha-shavua (weekly portion of the Torah) "Vayetzeh" about the time he arrived in Jerusalem: "How awful this place is, this is nothing but the House of G-d and this is the Gate of Heaven" (Bereshit, 28.17).
1 CONT
1 CONT His book "Shmot Gittin" deals with importance of monitoring carefully the correct spelling of names in the work done by sofrei gittin - scribes of divorce certificates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later, besides his glosses to his father's "Emek Brakha" (Cracow, 1597) and
1 CONT "Yesh Nohelin" (1617), R. Yishayahu wrote commentary to the book "Mordekhai"
1 CONT on the Talmudic tractate "Mo'ed", only a part of which was published in 1757
1 CONT under the title "Bigdei Yesha", chiddushim to "Moed", which constituted a
1 CONT response to "Chiddushei Ha-RITBA" and a commentary on the calendar of
1 CONT Mordekhai ben Hillel, a part of which was published together with "Emek Brakha" in 1787. Also his "Letters from Eretz Israel" which contain valuable autobiographical material, the article "Mitzvot Tefillin" and commentary on the "
1 CONC Zohar" have come down to us. Much biographical data is contained in the work of his son, Shabtai Sheftel, "Vavei Ha-Ammudim".
1 CONT
1 CONT Generations of Jews in Central and Eastern Europe until the end of 18th century walked in the light of the Holy SheLaH. It is very probable that this work influenced greatly the foundations of chassidism as conceived by BeShT. Man
1 CONC y of its tenets, such as transforming bad qualities into good ones and joy in everything one does became essential elements of the principles and way of life of that movement.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nowadays the grave of the Holy SheLah in Tiberias is visited by numerous
1 CONT believers, especially those who wish to find themselves a spouse or resolve the problems of education of their children. To this end they recite the wonderful prayer composed by R. Yishayahu. The tombstone on the grave, once witho
1 CONC ut any identifying inscription and later with one not befitting the greatness of the man, was repaired in 1999 by the Horowitz Families Association and a new inscription was made on its white marble:
1 CONT
1 CONT Here lies
1 CONT THE HOLY SheLaH
1 CONT Our teacher Rabbi YISHAYAHU ben Rabbi AVRAHAM HA-LEVI HOROWITZ
1 CONT Blessed be the memory of the righteous one
1 CONT Who served as head of the Rabbinical Court in the great communities of Europe
1 CONT Came from Prague to Eretz Israel in the year 5381 - 1621
1 CONT Served as rabbi in the communities of Jerusalem, Safed and Tiberias
1 CONT Composed the books "TWO TABLETS OF THE COVENANT" and others
1 CONT Passed away in Tiberias on the 21st of Nissan 5390
1 CONT May his soul be bound in the bond of life.
1 CONT
1 CONT FROM UNKNOWN INTERNET SITE:
1 CONT
1 CONT The Shloh Hakodosh
1 CONT
1 CONT The holy gaon, Reb. Yeshaya Halevi Horowitz was born in Lemberg in approximately the year 5330 (1570). His father R' Avrohom Halevi was dayan
1 CONT in Prague, Cracow and lastly in Lemberg. R' Yeshaya learnt with his father and with the gaon R' Shlomo Ben Reb Yehudah Leib zt"l, better known as the Maharshal Hasheini, the second Maharshal. He also learnt with the Maharam of
1 CONT Lublin, zt"l.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Chaya, daughter of one of the prominent members of the community
1 CONT in Vienna, Rabbi Avrohom Moyal. In an awesome statement, R' Sheftel zt"l
1 CONT later wrote about his mother, the Rebbetzin Chaya, "It was said in her generation that she lacked nothing in deeds and traits of the holy Imahos, Soroh, Rivka, Rochel and Leah."
1 CONT
1 CONT Over the years, R' Yeshaya Halevi practiced as rov in various communities: in 5360 (1600) in Dubno, Russia, in 5262 in Ostroa and from 5366 he took over the reins in Frankfurt, leading the prestigious kehilla until their expulsion
1 CONC  from the city on 27 Elul 5374 (1614). The rabbi was exiled together with his flock and he returned to Prague, where he was appointed Rov in 5375 (1615).
1 CONT
1 CONT When his Rebbetzin passed away on 4th Adar 5380 (1620), Reb Yeshaya Halevi
1 CONT decided to make his dream of going to live in Eretz Yisroel a reality. On 8th Elul 5381 the Shloh boarded the ship and after a turbulent, difficult journey he arrived in the holy land on 2nd Kislev, 5382. Upon reaching Yerushalayi
1 CONC m, he was immediately accepted as Chief Rabbi of Eretz Yisroel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Seeing the immense poverty of the settlers in the holy land, the Shloh sent
1 CONT messengers to the Diaspora and their Rabbonim, particularly to the communities where he had served as rov, initiating a tzedokoh campaign which he called "Yachatz" -- an acronym in Hebrew for the three cities Yerushalayim, Chevron
1 CONC  and Tzfas.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11th Elul, 5385 (1625), the Shloh was imprisoned due to a libel trumped up by the wicked Machmed Ibn Paroueh. On Rosh Hashonoh, start of the year 5286 he was freed and he fled to Tzfas and Teverioh. There he established his cen
1 CONC ter of learning and prayer in the ancient Beis Haknesses on the shores of the Kinneret in Teverioh.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was niftar on 11th Nisan and is buried in the old cemetery in Teverioh close to the grave of Rabbi Yochanan Ben Zakai.  His holy seforim were accepted and beloved by Jews the world over, a fact which is proven by the amount of 
1 CONC times his great sefer Shnei Luchos Habris had to be printed over and over.
1 CONT
1 CONT His famous siddur Shaar Hashomayim is also world renowned and in reference
1 CONT to it the Shach wrote in his approbation, "I guarantee that whoever davens with this siddur, his tefilloh will be accepted."
1 CONT
1 CONT The Shloh Hakodesh was extremely preoccupied with his Torah studies and duties as rov, and had no spare time at all. However, when he saw how most of the Jews of his time were grossly neglecting their fear of Hashem and their whol
1 CONC e attitude in serving Hashem was so lax, he took upon himself as a holy mission to teach Bnei Yisroel mussar, ethics and unadulterated fear of G-d. With this is mind, he wrote his holy sefer Shnei Luchos Habris, teaching the bypat
1 CONC hs of the Torah and exhorting every Rov and teacher to impress mussar and ways of serving Hashem upon their congregants and pupils.
1 CONT
1 CONT The greatness of this work of the Shloh Hakodesh and its value is indicated in the following amazing, if not chilling, story:
1 CONT
1 CONT The gaon of Frankfurt a generation later, Rabbi Yosef Shmuel zt"l, in addition to carrying out all his duties as rov of the city, bestowed the Torah world with his immense treasures. In the margins of his gemora next to each sugya
1 CONC , he would point out all the places where the gemora mentions this subject. This column, known as Masores Hashas has now been added to every gemora so that anyone studying a sugya has laid out in front of him a full index of every
1 CONC  place in Shas where the subject matter is mentioned.  Rabbi Yosef Shmuel had a wonderful method of learning Torah with a large group of the cream of talmidei chachomim of Frankfurt.
1 CONT
1 CONT All day they would each learn individually a chosen topic and in the evening
1 CONT they would gather together for the shiur of the rov. Each one would bring up for discussion the questions and comments that had arisen during the day's learning and a great and mighty storm would engulf the beis medrash. From all 
1 CONC directions questions and doubts were hurled with gale force and it seemed as though the sugya was too difficult to navigate with waves of problems that would drown those studying it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Until the rov Reb Yosef Shmuel opened his mouth and showed them how to steer a clear way through. He would answer and explain, prove and expound until the gemora radiated clarity, like the sun on a clear day. In this thorough mann
1 CONC er the group learned the whole of Shas over many years.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Chidoh, in his biography of R' Yosef Shmuel zt"l, wrote that the rov of Frankfurt learned all of Shas forty- two times, fulfilling the commandment "Vedibarto `bom'," (the numerical value of `bom' being forty- two). He also add
1 CONC ed that for 25 years he learnt standing on his feet and all his learning was Torah lishmoh!
1 CONT
1 CONT When this holy Rabbi, Reb Yosef Shmuel was niftar, all the city joined his levaya in great mourning and a long hesped was given.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon their return from the levaya, his group of talmidim gathered in the beis medrash to continue their practice of studying together and to their astonishment, in his seat, was their departed Rabbi, sitting ready to learn as in t
1 CONC he past. Shaken by the sight, they looked at one another, wondering if they were dreaming.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Do not be afraid and do not tremble," said R' Yosef Shmuel zt"l. "I will soon go back to my eternal rest. I came down to let you know what has been going on with me in the Heavenly court, up there in the World of Truth.  "When I 
1 CONC arrived to the Heavenly spheres they called out loud - - "make way for R' Yosef Shmuel" and immediately my court- case proceeded -- the verdict being that my place in Gan Eden is next to the Shloh Hakodesh. However, the Shloh hims
1 CONC elf arose in protest, refusing to accept the company of his new neighbor. "The reason," he said, "is as follows. I wrote the sefer Shnei Luchos Habris which is full of mussar, to teach all Jews ethics and the correct way to serve 
1 CONC Hashem. Many are the tears that I shed whilst compiling this sefer, entreating Hashem that it should fulfill its purpose in arousing the hearts of those who learn it to better their ways. However, this Reb Yosef Shmuel here spread
1 CONC  Torah to multitudes of people, learned with his distinguished group of talmidei chachomim all of Shas, yet never once did he teach them mussar or exhort them to better their avodas Hashem. How can he sit next to me here in Gan Ed
1 CONC en?"
1 CONT
1 CONT Reb Yosef Shmuel zt"l continued to relate to his mesmerized talmidim. "The
1 CONT Heavenly court heard out the Shloh and sat in judgment again. They decided that my rightful place, reward for my holy work all my years, cannot be taken away from me; however, the Shloh is correct in his argument that I did not te
1 CONC ach you mussar. They thus decreed that I must correct this misdeed before receiving my just reward and sent me down to you during our usual study hours to instruct you that from now and onwards before your daily learning session y
1 CONC ou should study mussar and avodas Hashem. In doing so, you will enable me to rest in peace and enjoy my reward in Gan Eden."
1 CONT
1 CONT Shaken by this heavenly revelation, the talmidim immediately took upon themselves strictly to obey his will, studying the Shloh's words of mussar every day before their shiur.
1 CONT
1 CONT With that their Rabbi disappeared, returning to his seat in Gan Eden next to the Shloh Hakodesh.
1 CONT
1 CONT Zechuso Yogen Oleinu.
