1 CONT G5.2. Dinah, known for her Jewish knowledge as an educated woman who  became known as "de Gedole" (the Great Woman). She was first married to Naftali Hirsch Katz, A.B.D. of Prusstiz, Nikolsburg (Moravia), Pinsk, and Lublin.  He wa
1 CONC s the progenitor of a very prominent Rabbinical family, Katz. His  ancestry is said to have dated back to the time of Eli the Priest and is recorded  in the book VeTzivah HaKohen, published at White Field 1823, by his  descendant 
1 CONC Aaron Samuel, son of Naftali Hirsch Katz. He was the author of Peirot Genosar, published Amsterdam 1742. At the time he was A.B.D. of Lublin, R. Abraham Joshua Heschel of Cracow was Head of the city's Yeshiva. Heschel  had been on
1 CONC e of the distinguished pupils of the MaHaRaM of Lublin, Meir ben Gedaliah (died 1616). Both these two sages gave their approbation to Damesek Eliezer by Rabbi Eliezer Samuel of Apt, published Lublin 1646, in 1645,  and the first a
1 CONC ppearance of the  Yoreh Deah of 1646. At the time of Naftali's death in 1649, R. Abraham Joshua had lost his first wife, the daughter of R. Moses Lazers of Brest. Dinah now cherished the hope of marrying this sage who had been acc
1 CONC ustomed to visit her home and having proposed to him, he married her and they had issue, besides those from their first marriages. She was buried at Brody and lies between the two daughters of R. Jacob Shor, her second cousins.
0 @N6000000003327772922@ NOTE
